<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="pl">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{% block title %}{{ 'page.default.header'|trans }}{% endblock %}</title>
    {% block meta %}{% endblock %}
    <link rel="icon" type="image/x-icon" href="{{ asset('images/favicon.ico') }}">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
    {{ include('partials/frontend-fonts.html.twig') }}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('styles/app.css') }}">
    {% block stylesheets %}
    {% endblock %}
</head>
{#
    Deliberately chrome-free: no top-menu (login state, user name), no
    footer, no mobile nav, no chat widget, no per-session notification
    tray. Every one of those varies by visitor, which would make the full
    HTML response uncacheable at a shared/CDN layer. This template renders
    byte-identical output for every visitor of a given URL, which is the
    precondition Stage 7's ETag/shared-cache work depends on.

    .site-shell is kept as the wrapping class (not the include) purely so
    the same CSS selector that scopes frontend typography in app.css
    (`.site-shell`, `.site-shell h1..h6`) keeps applying here too, without
    a second, duplicated set of font rules to maintain.
#}
<body class="bg-beige text-stone-800 antialiased">
<div class="site-shell min-h-screen">
    {% block body %}
    {% endblock %}
</div>
</body>
</html>
